course: dictionary
lesson: 145
title: Affect in context
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Affect is a verb, a doing word. Its forms are affect, affects, affected and affecting. To affect something means to have an effect on it, to change or influence it. «Rain affects the match» means the rain changes how the match goes.
explain: When affect means making someone feel strong emotions, the clue is in the feeling. «The sad story affected her deeply» tells you the story moved her a lot, so affected means touched or changed inside.
explain: Affect can also mean to attack or harm a part of the body. «The illness affects the lungs» tells you the illness is harming the lungs. Read the words around the word to decide which meaning fits.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping in a simpler word. «Too much screen time can affect sleep» becomes «Too much screen time can change sleep», which still makes sense, so affect means change or influence.
fact: Affect is a verb, so it is a doing word.
fact: «Affect» means to have an effect on something, to change or influence it.
fact: «Affect» can mean to make someone feel strong emotions, as in «deeply affected».
fact: «Affect» can mean to attack or harm a part of the body, as in «The illness affects the lungs».
fact: Formally, «affect» can mean to pretend, as in «affect an accent».
exercise: «Too much screen time can affect mood and sleep.» What does affect mean here? | To pretend to be someone else | To shout loudly | *To change or influence | To switch off and close
exercise: «The sad story affected her deeply.» What does affected mean here? | Stopped her from walking | *Made her feel strong emotions | Made her pretend to be sad | Made her laugh out loud
exercise: «The illness affects the lungs.» Find and copy the part of the body that is affected. | *lungs | illness | the | affects
test: «Pollution affects wildlife.» What does affect mean here? | *To have an effect on, and harm or change | To pretend | To make a loud noise | To tidy up
test: «She was affected by the news.» What does this mean? | She laughed at the news | *The news made her feel strong emotions | She wrote the news down | She ignored the news
exam: Which sentence uses affect to mean to pretend? | Lee wore his coat because it was cold. | *Lee began to affect a posh accent to make the class laugh. | Lee ran fast to catch the bus. | Lee ate his lunch at school.
